  push(P0(`Let *P*_{X} and *P*_{W} be the conditional expectations onto the functions of *X* and of *W*, orthogonal projections on L²(π), so that the numerator is ‖*F* − *P*_{X}*F*‖² + ‖*F* − *P*_{W}*F*‖² = ⟨*F*, (2*I* − *P*_{X} − *P*_{W})*F*⟩. If ρ(π) < 1, the ranges of *P*_{X} and *P*_{W} meet in the constants, a nonconstant function of *X* almost surely equal to a function of *W* having correlation one with it, and the cosine of the Friedrichs angle between them is ρ(π); in the decomposition of Lemma 8.5A, *P*_{X} + *P*_{W} has spectrum {1 ± cos θ} on the generic part, 1 on the two mixed summands and 0 on ker *P*_{X} ∩ ker *P*_{W}, and since *X* or *W* is not almost surely constant, the generic part or a mixed summand is not zero; so the spectrum of *P*_{X} + *P*_{W} off the constants has supremum 1 + ρ(π), and the infimum above is 1 − ρ(π). If ρ(π) = 1, either the two ranges share a nonconstant function, at which the quotient vanishes, or the cosine is one and the same decomposition makes the infimum zero.`, { before: 0 }));
  push(P(`Second, if π″ = *h*π with *a* ≤ *h* ≤ *b* for constants 0 < *a* ≤ *b*, then 1 − ρ(π″) ≥ (*a*/*b*)(1 − ρ(π)): indeed π″(Var_{π″}(*F* | *X*)) = inf_{c} π″((*F* − *c*(*X*))²) ≥ *a* inf_{c} π((*F* − *c*(*X*))²) = *a* π(Var_{π}(*F* | *X*)), the infimum over functions *c* of *X*, the same holds with *W*, and Var_{π″}(*F*) = inf_{c} π″((*F* − *c*)²) ≤ *b* Var_{π}(*F*); the first fact finishes.`));
  push(P(`Third, if π₁ = (*u* ⊗ *s*)π/π(*u* ⊗ *s*) for bounded functions *u* of *x* and *s* of *w* with positive infima, then ρ(π₁) ≤ (sup *u* · sup *s*/(inf *u* · inf *s*))^{1/2} ρ(π). Write β(*x*) = π(*s*(*W*) | *X* = *x*), ζ(*w*) = π(*u*(*X*) | *W* = *w*) and *Z* = π(*u* ⊗ *s*); the laws of *X* and *W* under π₁ have densities *u*β/*Z* and *s*ζ/*Z* with respect to those under π, so the maps *f* ↦ *f*(*u*β/*Z*)^{1/2} and *g* ↦ *g*(*s*ζ/*Z*)^{1/2} are unitary from L²(π₁^{X}) onto L²(π^{X}) and from L²(π₁^{W}) onto L²(π^{W}), and π₁(*f*(*X*)*g*(*W*)) = π(*a*₁*f̃*(*X*) · *a*₂*g̃*(*W*)) with *f̃*, *g̃* the images and *a*₁ = (*u*/β)^{1/2}, *a*₂ = (*s*/ζ)^{1/2}. Hence the operator *T*₁ of π₁, *T*₁*g* = π₁(*g*(*W*) | *X*), is unitarily equivalent to *a*₁*Ta*₂, *T* the operator of π and *a*₁, *a*₂ acting by multiplication. For each of these laws the operator maps the constants to the constants and the functions of mean zero to the functions of mean zero, so it is the orthogonal sum of the identity on the constants and its restriction to mean zero, whose norm is the maximal correlation; the maximal correlation is therefore the second singular number inf{‖*A* − *E*‖ : rank *E* ≤ 1} of the operator. Writing *T* = 1 ⊗ 1 + *T*₀ with ‖*T*₀‖ = ρ(π), the operator *a*₁*Ta*₂ is a rank-one operator plus *a*₁*T*₀*a*₂, so its second singular number, which is ρ(π₁), is at most ‖*a*₁‖_{∞}‖*a*₂‖_{∞}ρ(π); and β ≥ inf *s*, ζ ≥ inf *u* give ‖*a*₁‖_{∞}‖*a*₂‖_{∞} ≤ (sup *u* · sup *s*/(inf *u* · inf *s*))^{1/2}.`));
  push(P(`Now fix (*x*₀, *w*₀) and write δ(*x*, *w*) = δ(*x*, *w*₀) + (δ(*x*₀, *w*) − δ(*x*₀, *w*₀)) + δ₁₂(*x*, *w*), where δ₁₂(*x*, *w*) = (δ(*x*, *w*) − δ(*x*, *w*₀)) − (δ(*x*₀, *w*) − δ(*x*₀, *w*₀)) is at most Δ in modulus. The tilt by the first two terms is a tilt by *u* ⊗ *s* with sup *u*/inf *u* ≤ exp(*o*_{X}) and sup *s*/inf *s* ≤ exp(*o*_{W}), so the third fact gives ρ(π₁) ≤ exp((*o*_{X} + *o*_{W})/2) ρ(π); the tilt of π₁ by δ₁₂ has a density between two constants of ratio at most *e*^{2Δ}, so the second fact gives 1 − ρ(π′) ≥ *e*^{−2Δ}(1 − ρ(π₁)). Hence ρ(π′) ≤ *e*^{−2Δ}ρ(π₁) + 1 − *e*^{−2Δ} ≤ exp((*o*_{X} + *o*_{W})/2) ρ(π) + 1 − *e*^{−2Δ}. ∎[[fn:The second fact is the perturbation argument of R. Holley and D. Stroock, "Logarithmic Sobolev inequalities and stochastic Ising models", *J. Stat. Phys.* 46 (1987), 1159–1194, read for the two-site heat bath.]]`));
