  push(P(`Corollary 8.5F (ii) is the fourth link of §8.1 in the L² currency, with the inputs that (8.7) asks of the tilts in total variation: their oscillations and their weighted mixed differences tending to zero.`));
  push(P(`Theorem 8.5′ and the statements it rests on cost two conditions beyond those of Theorem 8.5, each used at a named place. First, (S) is used in the proofs only to identify ∩_{y} *H*_{y} with the constants, in Theorem 8.5D and through it in Theorem 8.5′, and to name the effective action that Corollary 8.5F (i) reads; for a block map whose blocked law is not of the form (S), Theorem 8.5D and Theorem 8.5′ hold verbatim with (S) replaced by that identification. Second, the passage from Hypothesis I to Hypothesis I′ uses translation covariance, through the equality of the weighted row and column sums of *C*^{λ}_{j}; without it, Hypothesis I′ holds with ½(α + ᾱ) in place of α, ᾱ a bound on the weighted column sums, which must satisfy α + ᾱ < 2. Nothing in the proofs of Theorem 8.5′, Theorem 8.5D, Propositions 8.4′, 8.5C and 8.5E, Corollary 8.5F, or Lemmas 8.5A and 8.5B uses that *v*_{y} is a function of the links of *B*_{y} alone, that the block map is a bijection of block configurations, or the value of *L*. Apart from Proposition 8.5E and Corollary 8.5F (i), whose subject is the matrix (8.5), none of them asks for a version of a conditional law beyond λ_{j}-almost every coarse configuration; Hypotheses I and II and the matrix (8.5) use versions defined for every coarse configuration.`));
  push(P(`Hypothesis I is read on the effective action of the coarse variables. Write the blocked measure as λ_{j}(*dv*) = *e*^{−A}*dv* with respect to the product over the blocks of a fixed reference measure on *V*, so that *A* = *A*_{j}(*v*) is the effective action at the coarse scale, and let Δ_{yy′}(*A*) be its mixed second difference in the sense of Proposition 8.4: the supremum, over coarse configurations differing only at *y*′, of the oscillation in *v*_{y} of the difference of the two actions. The conditional law of *v*_{y} under one of the two configurations is the tilt of its conditional law under the other by that difference, so the first inequality of Proposition 8.4 gives *C*^{λ}_{j}(*y*, *y*′) ≤ 1 − *e*^{−Δ} with Δ = Δ_{yy′}(*A*). Hypothesis I is therefore a bound on the mixed second differences of the effective action between blocks, weighted by their distance: the influence functional of §8.1, and the number that a certificate of the second link must deliver at the coarse scale.`, { before: 120 }));
  push(P(`One might wish to state the hypothesis on the fine measure directly, taking the blocks as the sites of a Gibbs measure and the whole configuration of a block as its variable, so that Dobrushin's condition for that measure would give every local observable's decay at once, with no fibre clause. That form is not uniform in the cutoff. Its interdependence coefficient between adjacent blocks is the total variation between two laws of all the links of a block under outside data that differ on a whole neighbouring face; as the spacing tends to zero that face carries ever more links, the two laws become distinguishable almost surely by an average over the face, and the coefficient tends to one at every coupling, so that the row sums tend to at least one. Whole-block total variation is the one norm that does not survive large blocks, and the hypothesis of the reduction is therefore stated on the coarse variables, whose type is fixed, with the field inside the blocks entering through its clustering alone.`, { before: 120 }));
  push(P(`Theorem 8.5 is the second link in the form the route attacks it. Hypothesis I is a bound on the blocked measure at the coarse scale, the object named in §8.1 as the influence functional of the blocked action. Hypothesis II is what the ultraviolet half must deliver below that scale; for the gauge group SU(2) the literature states nothing in this form, so it stands beside Hypothesis I as the second half of what the crossover must supply, and it is named as such. Proposition 8.4 is the fourth link in its exact form. The blocked measures λ_{j} have variables of one type at every rung, so it is meaningful to say that their conditional laws at the coarse scale are tilts of those of one rung *j*₁ by functions δ_{j} whose oscillations *o*_{j} and weighted mixed differences Σ_{y′}(1 − *e*^{−Δ})*e*^{κ|y−y′|} tend to zero; when they are, Hypothesis I at the rung *j*₁ with the constant α₁ implies it at every later rung with any α ∈ (α₁, 1), by (8.7). That the measures stand in this relation is the ultraviolet interface of §8.1, and it is the place at which Bałaban's theorem enters the chain; the two statements the corpus leaves unwritten there, and the discharge of the theorem's own hypothesis on the sequence of couplings, are theorems on this work's path (§8.3).`));
  push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured, and the crossover is crossed with certified numbers. Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every character coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and has certified a bound on the Dobrushin row of the flowed weight *F*ₖ placed on ℤ⁴ as the weight of every plaquette, at every step from β = 2.2 and β = 2.3 and from the eleventh step from β = 5 and the thirty-first from β = 10; the earlier steps from those two couplings the certificate refuses by name, the eight-term truncation leaving its lower bound on *F*ₖ non-positive near θ = π. Write *F*ₖ(*U*) = exp(βₖ cos θ + δₖ(θ)) for *U* of angle θ, with βₖ the Wilson coupling whose fundamental character coefficient is the step's own, so that δₖ is the weight's departure from Wilson's form. A weight tilted by e^{δ} keeps Dobrushin's interdependence up to the factor e^{o} and adds 1 − e^{−Δ} for each neighbour, where o bounds the oscillation of the tilt along the varied link and Δ its mixed difference between the two links;[[fn:The two tilt lemmas: a probability law tilted by e^{ρ} with ρ of oscillation at most o moves by at most 1 − e^{−o} in total variation, and two laws tilted by the same ρ keep their distance up to the factor e^{o}; the triangle inequality then gives the tilted interdependence e^{o}C + (1 − e^{−Δ}). Both proofs are short and hold on any probability space; the finite form is checked by the Lean kernel in CrossoverCriterion.lean (Records).]] each link lies in six plaquettes and has eighteen neighbouring links, two distinct links share at most one plaquette, and the Wilson part's row is 18 · 4βₖ/(3π) by §8.1, so the row is at most *B*ₖ = e^{6 osc δₖ} · (24/π) βₖ + 18 (1 − e^{−2 osc δₖ}), which is the single link's disk βₖ < 3π/72 of §8.1 when δₖ = 0. From β = 2.2 and from β = 2.3 the budget closes at the eighth step, B₈ ∈ [0.033548, 0.033599] and B₈ ∈ [0.28150, 0.28192], after B₇ ∈ [2.0760, 2.0791] and B₇ ∈ [4.9347, 4.9420]; from β = 5 it closes at the nineteenth, B₁₉ ∈ [0.026779, 0.026820] after B₁₈ ∈ [1.9154, 1.9182]; and from β = 10 at the thirty-ninth, B₃₉ ∈ [0.0055722, 0.0055806] after B₃₈ ∈ [1.1355, 1.1372], where the coupling β₃₈ = 0.1177 already lies inside the single link's disk and the departure from Wilson's form alone holds the budget above one. The eighth iterate from the crossover coupling therefore defines on ℤ⁴ a theory with a unique infinite-volume Gibbs measure and exponential clustering, and, its character coefficients being non-negative so that the weight is reflection positive, a lattice mass gap by Theorem 7.1; and eight is the first step at which the budget closes, the budget itself being certified above one at the step before. The hierarchical model's own confinement at every coupling, with lower bounds on its string tension and mass gap, is classical,[[fn:K. R. Ito, "Permanent quark confinement in four-dimensional hierarchical lattice gauge theories of Migdal–Kadanoff type", *Phys. Rev. Lett.* 55 (1985), 558; V. F. Müller and J. Schiemann, "Convergence of Migdal–Kadanoff iterations: a simple and general proof", *Lett. Math. Phys.* 15 (1988), 289–295, whose rate of convergence gives lower bounds on the string tension and the mass gap of the hierarchical four-dimensional gauge models. Ito's theorem covers the hierarchical U(N) models, U(1) among them.]] and what the certificate adds is the number of steps and the budget at each of them: on the hierarchical model eight blocking steps carry the crossover coupling into the disk, each one certified.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent and the certified budget; the instruments, the records at the four starting couplings and the known answers are listed in the Records. The first step from β = 2.2 is read from the same record: the coefficient rises from 0.464 to 0.486, an effective coupling of 2.35, where the one-loop running of ℤ⁴ takes 2.2 to 1.94 under one doubling of the scale. Revision 30 counted instead the steps at which the coefficient c₁ itself falls below 0.131, the seventh from β = 2.3, the eighteenth from β = 5 and the thirty-seventh from β = 10, and compared them with a bound that §8.1 states on the coupling: the disk is β < 3π/72, c₁ < 0.0327 for a weight of Wilson's form, and the budget above, which also prices the departure from that form, closes one or two steps after the coefficient's crossing.]] The record also says what the model is not. Its first step from β = 2.2 moves the coupling up where the running of ℤ⁴ moves it down, by an amount of the step's own size, so its first steps are not ℤ⁴'s: the distance Hypotheses I and II must cross is counted in the blocking steps of ℤ⁴, and the hierarchical count is the measured model of it. One more thing is known about the inequality that would carry the model's control to ℤ⁴. A comparison that bounded the mixed second differences of the effective action of ℤ⁴ by those of the hierarchical model, and held for every compact gauge group, would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap wherever its hierarchical flow reaches the disk; the hierarchical U(1) model confines at every coupling by the same theorem, its flow descending at a rate exponentially small in the coupling, 7.3·10⁻⁵ of β per step at an effective coupling of 1.39 and about 3·10⁻¹³ at 2.83, where the SU(2) flow descends by 0.256 to 0.258 per step near β = 5.5, the rate of ℤ⁴'s one-loop running, 0.2575 per doubling; and the U(1) theory on ℤ⁴ has a massless phase at weak coupling.[[fn:A. H. Guth, "Existence proof of a nonconfining phase in four-dimensional U(1) lattice gauge theory", *Phys. Rev. D* 21 (1980), 2291–2307; J. Fröhlich and T. Spencer, "Massless phases and symmetry restoration in abelian gauge theories and spin systems", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 83 (1982), 411–454.]] The inequality the step needs therefore depends on the group, and that dependence is the first property a proof of Hypothesis I must use; on the hierarchical model the two groups part in the rate of descent, which is where the running coupling lives.`));
  push(P(`Two things are known about the strong end of that distance. With the first-order transmission between links, the total variation moved by one neighbour's change, no block criterion of Dobrushin and Shlosman's kind gains on the single link at any block side, because a link deep inside a block branches to its eighteen neighbours exactly as a link of the full lattice does, and the criterion's series has the single link's ratio. A block gains only through the transmission across an integrated face, which carries the product of the two pinned means of the face's other edges, an average over the interior: its one-face constant is an explicit function of the moments of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere, the covariance of the transmitted variable being a multiple of the identity plus one rank-one term whose size is a closed form in two Bessel ratios, certified in a run of record. What is not in hand is the theorem that composes that transmission along paths inside a block: every composition that is proved, Dobrushin's comparison theorem among them, takes a supremum over configurations at each step and so composes the first-order sensitivities only, which return the single link's disk; a convergent expansion of the block measure would compose the averages, and its radius has no proof beyond that disk.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the block criterion's accounting, the one-face law and the closed form of its covariance are entered on the ledger of the work that follows this paper, with their readings on the exact cube.]] The paper carries no number for that widening until both are theorems.`));
  push(P(`One averaged composition is rigorous, and it is an instance of Theorem 8.5 in which the fibre clause holds exactly. Call a set of links of ℤ⁴ a *perfect cover* when every plaquette contains exactly one of its links. One exists with period two in every direction: reading the coordinates of a site *x* modulo 2, take the link from *x* in direction 1 when *x*₂ = *x*₃ = *x*₄, in direction 2 when *x*₄ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₃, in direction 3 when *x*₂ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₄, and in direction 4 when *x*₃ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₂. The four conditions partition the sixteen residues, so the set has one link at every site and density one quarter, and a direct check of the ninety-six plaquettes of the torus of side two shows that each contains exactly one of its links; since the condition is local and the set periodic, the same holds on ℤ⁴ and on every torus of even side.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the cover was found by an exhaustive search on the torus of side two and checked again, on a road sharing no line with the search, on every plaquette of the tori of side two, four and six, where a single bent site leaves ten plaquettes of the smallest torus with zero or two links of the set; Records R7.]] Two consequences are used below: no plaquette contains two links of the cover, and a link outside the cover lies in six plaquettes whose six cover links are distinct, since two links share at most one plaquette.`));
  push(Thm('Lemma 8.6', 'The constants of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere', [
    `For w in ℝ⁴ let ν_{w} be the law on the unit sphere S³ of ℝ⁴, identified with SU(2), whose density with respect to the uniform measure is proportional to e^{⟨u, w⟩}. Write r = |w|, Z(r) = 2I₁(r)/r for its normalising constant, φ = log Z and m = φ′ = I₂/I₁, with I_{ν} the modified Bessel functions. The mean of ν_{w} is m(r)ŵ; its covariance has the eigenvalue φ″(r) on ŵ and m(r)/r on the orthogonal complement; and its third cumulant along a unit vector u with ⟨u, ŵ⟩ = c is φ‴(r)c³ + 3(D(r)/r)c(1 − c²), where D = φ″ − m/r. For every r ≥ 0:`,
    `(i) m(r)/r ≤ 1/4 and φ″(r) ≤ 1/4;`,
    `(ii) −m(r)²/3 ≤ D(r) ≤ 0;`,
    `(iii) the third cumulant of ν_{w} along every unit vector is at most m(r)/2 in absolute value.`,
  ]));
  push(Proof([
    `The cumulants of ν_{w} are the derivatives at *w* of *x* ↦ φ(|*x*|) − φ(|*w*|), the logarithm of the normalised Laplace transform of ν_{w}. Its gradient at *w* is *m*ŵ and its Hessian has the stated eigenvalues; along the line *x* + *tu* the radius ρ(*t*) = |*x* + *tu*| has ρ′ = *c*, ρ″ = (1 − *c*²)/*r* and ρ‴ = −3*c*(1 − *c*²)/*r*² at *t* = 0, so the third derivative φ‴ρ′³ + 3φ″ρ′ρ″ + φ′ρ‴ is the stated form. The recurrences *I*₁′ = *I*₂ + *I*₁/*r* and *I*₂′ = *I*₁ − 2*I*₂/*r* give φ′ = *m* and the Riccati identity`,
  ], 'Proof', true));
  push(Eq([mr('m′ = 1 − '), frac('3m', 'r'), mr(' − '), sup('m', '2'), mr('.')], '8.9'));
  push(P0(`Put *z* = *r*²/4 and *A* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 2)!), *B* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 1)!), *C* = Σ_{k} *z*^{k}/(*k*!(*k* + 3)!), so that *A* = (2/*r*)²*I*₂, *B* = (2/*r*)*I*₁ and *C* = (2/*r*)³*I*₃, all three positive, with *m*/*r* = *A*/(2*B*) and *m*² = *zA*²/*B*². Comparing coefficients gives *B* − 2*A* = *zC* ≥ 0, which is the first half of (i). By (8.9), φ″ = 1 − 3*A*/(2*B*) − *zA*²/*B*², and the substitution *B* = 2*A* + *zC* gives`));
  push(Eq([frac('1', '4'), mr(' − φ″ = '), frac(mr('z (A² − ¾ BC)'), mr('B²')), mr(',      D = φ″ − '), frac('m', 'r'), mr(' = − '), frac(mr('z (A² − BC)'), mr('B²')), mr('.')], '8.10'));
  push(P0(`Now *A*² − *BC* = (2/*r*)⁴(*I*₂² − *I*₁*I*₃). The product formula *I*_{μ}*I*_{ν} = Σ_{k} (*r*/2)^{μ+ν+2k} (μ + ν + 2*k*)!/(*k*!(μ + *k*)!(ν + *k*)!(μ + ν + *k*)!)[[fn:G. N. Watson, *A Treatise on the Theory of Bessel Functions*, second edition, Cambridge University Press, 1944, §5.41.]] makes the *k*-th coefficient of *I*₂² − *I*₁*I*₃ equal to (4 + 2*k*)!/(*k*!(4 + *k*)!(2 + *k*)!(3 + *k*)!), which is positive, and that of 3*I*₁*I*₃ − 2*I*₂² equal to *k*/(2 + *k*) times the *k*-th coefficient of *I*₁*I*₃, which is not negative. Hence 0 ≤ *A*² − *BC* ≤ *A*²/3. The left inequality is Turán's inequality for the modified Bessel functions.[[fn:K. V. Thiruvenkatachar and T. S. Nanjundiah, "Inequalities concerning Bessel functions and orthogonal polynomials", *Proc. Indian Acad. Sci. A* 33 (1951), 373–384.]] By (8.10) it gives *D* ≤ 0, and, since then ¾*BC* ≤ ¾*A*² ≤ *A*², it gives φ″ ≤ 1/4, the second half of (i). The right inequality gives |*D*| ≤ *zA*²/(3*B*²) = *m*²/3, which is (ii). For (iii), differentiate (8.9): φ‴ = 3|*D*|/*r* − 2*m*φ″, where 0 ≤ 3|*D*|/*r* ≤ *m*²/*r* ≤ *m*/4 by (ii) and (i), and 0 ≤ 2*m*φ″ ≤ *m*/2 because φ″ is a variance at most 1/4. With *a* = 3|*D*|/*r* and *b* = 2*m*φ″, the third cumulant at *c* in [0, 1] is *ac*(2*c*² − 1) − *bc*³, and it is odd in *c*. When 2*c*² ≥ 1 it lies between −*bc*³ ≥ −(*m*/2)*c*³ and *ac*(2*c*² − 1) ≤ (*m*/4)*c*(2*c*² − 1); when 2*c*² < 1 it equals −(*ac*(1 − 2*c*²) + *bc*³), whose size is at most (*m*/4)*c*(1 − 2*c*²) + (*m*/2)*c*³ = (*m*/4)*c*. In every case its size is at most (*m*/4)*c*(1 + *c*²) ≤ *m*/2. ∎`));
  push(Thm('Theorem 8.7', 'A lattice mass gap at strong coupling; Edwin Boston III', [
    `Consider the SU(2) lattice gauge theory on ℤ⁴ with Wilson's action, each plaquette weighted by exp(½ β tr U_{p}), at 0 < β ≤ 0.241. Its infinite-volume Gibbs measure is unique. For every m₀ < log(1/α(β))/2, with α(β) ≤ 0.9963 the number of (8.12), there is a constant K, depending on β and m₀ alone, such that for all bounded observables f and g depending on finitely many links, |⟨fg⟩ − ⟨f⟩⟨g⟩| ≤ K N(f) N(g) ‖f‖ ‖g‖ e^{−m₀ d}, with N(f) the number of links on which f depends and d the least distance, in the maximum norm, between the midpoints of a link of f and a link of g; the same bound holds on every torus of even side. The Hamiltonian H = −log T of the transfer matrix T annihilates a unique vacuum and has no spectrum in the interval (0, m₀).`,
  ]));
  push(Proof([
    `Let *L*₁ be the perfect cover and *L*₀ its complement, and read each element of SU(2) as a unit vector of ℝ⁴, so that ½ tr *U*_{p} = ⟨*U*_{a}, *S*_{p}(*a*)⟩ for the cover link *a* of a plaquette *p*, the staple *S*_{p}(*a*) being the product of the other three links of *p*, or of their inverses, read as a unit vector. Each plaquette contains exactly one link of *L*₁, so the Wilson weight is the product over *a* in *L*₁ of exp⟨*U*_{a}, η(*a*)⟩, with η(*a*) = β Σ_{p∋a} *S*_{p}(*a*) and |η(*a*)| ≤ 6β. Given the links of *L*₀, the links of *L*₁ are therefore independent, *U*_{a} distributed by ν_{w} at *w* = η(*a*), and the marginal law of the links of *L*₀ has density proportional to ∏_{a∈L₁} *Z*(|η(*a*)|) with respect to the product of the Haar measures: a Gibbs measure with a potential of finite range. For *x* in *L*₀ and a plaquette *p* ∋ *x*, let *a*_{p} be the cover link of *p*, θ_{p}(*U*_{x}) = β*S*_{p}(*a*_{p}), which is β times an isometry of ℝ⁴ applied to *U*_{x}, and *w*_{p} = η(*a*_{p}) − θ_{p}(*U*_{x}), the contribution of the five other plaquettes of *a*_{p}, so that |*w*_{p}| ≤ 5β. The conditional law of *U*_{x} given the other links of *L*₀ is proportional to ∏_{p∋x} *Z*(|*w*_{p} + θ_{p}(*U*_{x})|), the six factors coming from six distinct cover links.`,
    `By Taylor's theorem along the segment from *w*_{p} to *w*_{p} + θ_{p}, which stays in the ball of radius 6β, and since *m* is increasing, log *Z*(|*w*_{p} + θ_{p}|) = φ(|*w*_{p}|) + ⟨*m*(|*w*_{p}|)ŵ_{p}, θ_{p}⟩ + ½⟨θ_{p}, Σ_{p}θ_{p}⟩ + *R*_{p}, with Σ_{p} the covariance of ν_{w} at *w* = *w*_{p} and |*R*_{p}| ≤ (β³/6)*T*₆, *T*₆ = *m*(6β)/2, by Lemma 8.6(iii). The linear terms add to ⟨*U*_{x}, *v*⟩ for a vector *v* to which *p* contributes a vector of length at most β*m*(5β); the quadratic term of *p* equals a constant plus ½β²*D*(|*w*_{p}|)⟨θ_{p}/β, ŵ_{p}⟩², whose oscillation in *U*_{x} is at most ½β²*D*₅, with *D*₅ = *m*(5β)²/3, by Lemma 8.6(ii). The conditional law of *U*_{x} is therefore the tilt of ν_{v} by a function *h* = Σ_{p} *h*_{p} of oscillation at most`,
  ], 'Proof', true));
  push(Eq([mr('ω = 3'), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(' + 2'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr('.')], '8.11'));
  push(P0(`Two facts bound the influence of one link on that law. First, if *p* is a probability measure and *g* a bounded function of oscillation *o*, the tilt *T*_{g}*p* differs from *p* in total variation by at most tanh(*o*/4) ≤ *o*/4: with *g* normalised to take values in [0, *o*], for a set *B* of *p*-measure *s* on which *e*^{g} averages *a* and off which it averages *a*′, (*T*_{g}*p*)(*B*) − *p*(*B*) = *s*(1 − *s*)(*a* − *a*′)/(*sa* + (1 − *s*)*a*′), which is largest at *a* = *e*^{o}, *a*′ = 1 and *s* = 1/(1 + *e*^{o/2}), where it equals tanh(*o*/4). Second, by the sharp one-link lemma of §8.1, the mean absolute deviation of ⟨*U*, *e*⟩ under any law ν_{v} is at most 4/(3π) for every unit vector *e*, and under a tilt of ν_{v} by a function of oscillation at most ω it is at most (3*e*^{ω} − 1)/2 times as large: if the tilt has density *g* with values in [*c*, *ce*^{ω}] with respect to ν_{v}, where *c* ≤ 1, and *Y* = ⟨*U*, *e*⟩ − ν_{v}(⟨*U*, *e*⟩) with *M* = ν_{v}(|*Y*|), the deviation is at most ν_{v}(*g*|*Y*|) + |ν_{v}(*gY*)| ≤ *ce*^{ω}*M* + *c*(*e*^{ω} − 1)*M*/2, because ν_{v}(*Y*⁺) = *M*/2. Differentiating along the segment from *v* to *v*′, two tilts of ν_{v} and ν_{v′} by one function of oscillation at most ω differ in total variation by at most ρ(ω)(2/(3π))|*v* − *v*′|, with ρ(ω) = (3*e*^{ω} − 1)/2.`));
  push(P(`Now let a link *y* of *L*₀ change, the others fixed. If *y* is one of the two other links of a plaquette *p* of *x*, as twelve links are, then *v* moves by at most 2β*m*(5β), because a factor of the isometry moves by at most 2, and *h*_{p} moves by a function of oscillation at most β²*D*₅ + (2/3)β³*T*₆. If *y* lies in one of the five other plaquettes of a cover link *a*_{p}, as ninety links do, fifteen for each of the six, then *w*_{p} moves by at most 2β, so *v* moves by at most β·¼·2β = β²/2, the Jacobian of the mean being the covariance, whose norm is at most ¼ by Lemma 8.6(i); the quadratic part of *h*_{p} moves by a function of oscillation at most min(β²*D*₅, 2β³*T*₆), the second bound because the covariance moves in norm by at most 2β times the bound on the third cumulant, the norm of a symmetric trilinear form being its supremum on the diagonal; and the remainder moves by at most (2/3)β³*T*₆. By the two facts the interdependence coefficients are at most`));
  push(Eq([sub('C', 'dir'), mr(' = ρ(ω) '), frac('4β', '3π'), mr(' m(5β) + '), frac('1', '4'), mr('( '), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(' + '), frac('2', '3'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(' ),')]));
  push(Eq([sub('C', 'med'), mr(' = ρ(ω) '), frac(sup('β', '2'), '3π'), mr(' + '), frac('1', '4'), mr('( min('), sup('β', '2'), sub('D', '5'), mr(', 2'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(') + '), frac('2', '3'), sup('β', '3'), sub('T', '6'), mr(' ),      α(β) = 12 '), sub('C', 'dir'), mr(' + 90 '), sub('C', 'med'), mr('.')], '8.12'));
  push(P0(`A link *y* that occupies several of these positions is counted once for each, which bounds its coefficient because total variation is subadditive along a chain of single changes, and no other link of *L*₀ affects the law of *U*_{x}; the column sums obey the same count. Every term of α is increasing in β, and Table 3 gives its value: α(0.241) = 0.996297 < 1. By Dobrushin's uniqueness theorem the Gibbs measure of the links of *L*₀ is unique,[[fn:R. L. Dobrushin, "Prescribing a system of random variables by conditional distributions", *Theory Probab. Appl.* 15 (1970), 458–486; Sources [17].]] and, since the links of *L*₁ are conditionally independent given them with a law that is fixed, so is the Gibbs measure of the theory. The coefficients vanish between links at distance more than two, so the weighted row sums of Hypothesis I are at most α*e*^{2κ}, which is less than one for κ < log(1/α)/2; Hypothesis II holds with zero fibre covariance for observables with disjoint supports, whose links in *L*₁ are distinct and conditionally independent, and with fibre means that depend on the links of *L*₀ within distance one of their support. The argument of the proof of Theorem 8.5, applied on one lattice with the links of *L*₀ as the coarse variables, gives the clustering bound with any *m*₀ < log(1/α)/2, uniformly over the tori of even side and in the infinite volume. The Wilson action is reflection positive and its transfer matrix is strictly positive,[[fn:K. Osterwalder and E. Seiler, Sources [5]; M. Lüscher, Sources [7].]] the bound applies to θ*f̄* and τ_{t}*f* at every integer time *t*, and Theorem 7.1, applied to the reconstruction at integer times, gives the vacuum and the gap. ∎`));
